      {
        "id": "p54-q22-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "Find the coordinates of the two points of inflection.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(-1,\\ln2)$ and $(1,\\ln2)$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(1-x^2)}{(1+x^2)^2}&=0\\\\1-x^2&=0\\\\x&=\\pm1,\\\\y(\\pm1)&=\\ln(1+1)\\\\&=\\ln2.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe denominator of $y''$ is positive. For $x<-1$, $y''<0$; for $-1<x<1$, $y''>0$; for $x>1$, $y''<0$. Concavity changes at both values, so the points of inflection are $(-1,\\ln2)$ and $(1,\\ln2)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      },
      {
        "id": "p54-q22-g",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "g",
        "prompt": "Hence sketch the curve, and then write down its range.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "See sketch. Range: $y\\geq0$.",
          "worked": "The curve is symmetric about the $y$-axis, decreases to its minimum at $(0,0)$, then increases. It is concave up for $-1<x<1$ and concave down for $x<-1$ and $x>1$, with inflection points $(\\pm1,\\ln2)$. As $x\\to\\pm\\infty$, $y\\to\\infty$. Hence its range is $[0,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": [
